<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Kōya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded 819</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Kōya-san, Koyasan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Kōya is a large temple settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama-prefecture">Wakayama Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, and the headquarters of the Kōyasan sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shingon-buddhism">Shingon Buddhism</a>, whose head temple is Kongōbu-ji. It was first settled in 819 by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a>, on an 800-metre plain among eight peaks said to resemble a lotus. The original monastery has grown into the town of Kōya, with a university of religious studies and 120 sub-temples, many of which lodge pilgrims. Its Okunoin holds Kūkai's mausoleum within the largest graveyard in Japan, and the third shōgun Iemitsu built a tomb there for the Tokugawa family. In 2004 it became part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-koya.jpg" alt="Mount Kōya" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danjogaran_Koyasan12n3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mount Kōya, Kōya-san in Japanese, is a large temple settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama-prefecture">Wakayama Prefecture</a> to the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. In the strictest sense the name is the mountain name, or sangō, of Kongōbu-ji Temple, the ecclesiastical headquarters of the Kōyasan sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shingon-buddhism">Shingon Buddhism</a>. The monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a> first settled it in 819. He chose a plain 800 metres high among eight peaks because the terrain was supposed to resemble a lotus plant, and the original monastery has since grown into the town of Kōya.</p><p>The settlement has a university dedicated to religious studies and 120 sub-temples, many of which offer lodging to pilgrims. It is also a common starting point for the Shikoku Pilgrimage associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a>. Kongōbu-ji, the head temple, stands roughly in the middle of the sanctuary and is colloquially known as Kōyasan-Issan, the mountain of Kōya. The warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> built it for his mother's benefit when she died, and it was originally named Seigan-ji.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the middle of the settlement lies the Danjō Garan, an area of the main sacred buildings: a main hall, several pagodas, a scripture storage, a bell tower and a lecture hall. The Konpon Daitō, or Basic Great Pagoda, stands 48.5 metres tall in the middle of Kōyasan. According to Shingon doctrine it represents the central point of a mandala covering all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and it was built as a seminary for the esoteric practices of the sect. The main gate, the Daimon, is flanked on each side by Kongō warriors who guard the mountain.</p><p>Okunoin is the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a>, surrounded by the largest graveyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. His spirit is considered to be alive and is treated as a living deity, attended daily by Buddhist priests. The Tokugawa family tomb was built by the third shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a>, took ten years to build and is architecturally representative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>; the first shōgun, Ieyasu, is enshrined on its right and the second, Hidetada, on its left.</p><p>In 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated Kongōbu-ji on Mount Kōya as part of the World Heritage Site Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range, and the Kōya Sankeimichi pilgrimage route was inscribed with it. Visitors usually come by the Nankai Electric Railway from Namba Station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> to Gokurakubashi, then by a cable car that takes five minutes. Many monasteries on the mountain serve as lodgings, with an evening meal and breakfast, and guests may join the morning services.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Kōya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shingon, brought by the monk Kūkai, who founded the monastery of <b>Mount Kōya</b> in 819, worked through symbols, rituals, incantations and mandalas.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
